<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Athletes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify messages for peers. Concept: Clarify misunderstandings in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify messages for peers by using gestures and actions to convey meaning (e.g., says, "He's a singer." and pretends to hold a microphone and sing to represent the singer Sech). Can clarify misunderstandings in group discussions with simple gestures (e.g., mimics jumping moves and says "Irving Saladino was a long jumper."). Can use visual aids to simplify achievements of a famous Panamanian (e.g., points to medals and says, "He won many awards.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays pictures of three famous Panamanian athletes (e.g., Mariano Rivera, Atheyna Bylon, Irving Saladino). The teacher points to each picture and asks, "Who is this athlete?" Students respond. Then, the teacher asks, "What is one thing they are famous for?" The teacher encourages students to use gestures to show their ideas if they cannot find the words easily, activating prior knowledge and introducing the concept of non-verbal mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of Mariano Rivera and says, "Mariano Rivera is a very famous baseball player. He achieved many awards, like winning the World Series multiple times. He was the best closer!" The teacher then pretends to throw a baseball. The teacher then says, "Now, how can I simplify this for someone who doesn't know baseball? I can say, 'He plays baseball!' and show a throwing action." The teacher models simplifying a description and using a gesture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I showed the throwing action, did it help you understand what Mariano Rivera does?" (Checks understanding of gestures as mediation)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'He plays baseball!' a simpler message than 'He achieved many awards, like winning the World Series multiple times'?" (Checks understanding of simplifying messages)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue between two Panamanian teenagers discussing their "Biography Timeline" projects for homework, focusing on famous athletes. The dialogue includes brief descriptions of athletes and some clarifications.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing achievements of celebrities and developing reports on notable figures. Clarifying misunderstandings in group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher tells students they will listen to two friends talking about their project. "Pay attention to how they explain things to each other."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio dialogue:</li>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Student A:* "My project is on Atheyna Bylon. She's a boxer. She won a medal in the Olympic Games."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Student B:* "Oh, an Olympic Games? Like running?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Student A:* "No, no. Not running. She is a boxer! *[mimics punching lightly]* She achieved a medal in boxing competitions. She is training for a new tournament next year. She is very skilled."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Student B:* "Ah, I get it now. Boxing. And she won an award! My project is about Roberto Durán. He is more famous than many other boxers. He is considered the best."</li>
                        </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist questions):</b></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What project are the students talking about?" (Biography Timeline)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who are the two athletes mentioned?" (Atheyna Bylon and Roberto Durán)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did Student A do to help Student B understand 'boxer'?" (Used a gesture)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice simplifying messages, clarifying misunderstandings using gestures/visuals, and applying comparatives/superlatives, past simple, and present continuous for future. They use vocabulary like <i>athlete, achievement, award, win, inspire, train, compete, famous, popular, skilled, more, best</i>. Focus on pronunciation of -ed/-ing endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Simplify and Show (Controlled Practice)</b></li>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays sentences on the board about famous Panamanians. For example: "Sech is an incredibly popular Panamanian entertainer who performs music for many fans." Students work in pairs to simplify the message and demonstrate one key action with a gesture. (e.g., "Sech is a famous singer." *[holds imaginary microphone and sways]*)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other examples: "Mariano Rivera became one of the greatest relief pitchers of all time." (Simplify: "Mariano Rivera is the best baseball player." *[throws a ball]*) "Irving Saladino competed in long jump at the Olympics and won a gold medal." (Simplify: "Irving Saladino was a long jumper." *[mimics jumping]*)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors for correct simplification and appropriate gestures. Focus on pronunciation of '-ing' in 'competing,' 'winning,' 'training.'</li>
                        </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Clarify the Clues (Semi-controlled Practice)</b></li>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive cards with sentences about athletes, but with a potential "misunderstanding" or a complex term. For example: "Atheyna Bylon ___ a medal in the Olympic Games." (Students fill in 'won').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another example: "Pedro Poveda is a ___ actor, but he is ___ popular than Rubén Blades." (Students fill in 'talented', 'less' or 'not as').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, Student A reads the sentence, and Student B pretends not to fully understand a part. Student A must clarify the word/phrase using simpler words, a gesture, or an example. Example: Student A: "Atheyna Bylon won a medal." Student B: "What's a medal?" Student A: "It's an award for an achievement. Like a shiny prize!" *[points to chest like wearing a medal]*</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher prompts students to use the target grammar (comparatives, past simple) and vocabulary in their clarifications. Emphasize clear pronunciation of '-ed' endings in 'won,' 'achieved,' 'performed.'</li>
                        </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Timeline Snapshot (Pair Interaction / Peer Check)</b></li>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take out their "Biography Timeline" project notes (from previous lessons). In pairs, they take turns presenting one key achievement or fact from their chosen Panamanian's life.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A presents one fact (e.g., "Mariano Rivera won many awards. He is considered the best closer."). Student B acts as a peer who needs clarification, asking questions like "What does 'closer' mean?" or "What kind of awards?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A must mediate the information: simplify, explain using gestures, or point to any visual aids they have for their project. Student B provides feedback on clarity.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, listening for effective mediation, correct use of grammar (e.g., "He is training for a new tournament," "She was more talented"), and pronunciation of inflectional endings.</li>
                        </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Presentation of "Biography Timeline" project with a peer mediation focus.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each student presents their "Biography Timeline" project about a famous Panamanian athlete (or other figure, if they chose an entertainer in earlier lessons).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When a student presents, the other group members act as their "audience." They are instructed to ask clarifying questions about any information that is unclear or complex. They can pretend they don't know the person or the sport.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The presenter's main task is to mediate the information:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Simplify complex sentences about achievements (e.g., "He won many awards," "She is more famous than other athletes.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use gestures and actions to convey meaning (e.g., miming a sport, pretending to hold an award).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use visual aids from their timeline (pictures, drawings of medals) to support their explanations.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarify any misunderstandings expressed by their peers.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes turns presenting and mediating. The teacher circulates, observing mediation strategies and listening for target grammar (past simple, comparatives, present continuous for future), vocabulary (athlete, achievement, award, inspire, etc.), and pronunciation of inflectional endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example interaction: Student A: "My Panamanian is Irving Saladino. He was a long jumper. He won a gold award in the Olympics." Student B: "Long jumper? What's that?" Student A: "It means he jumped very far! *[mimics a long jump action]* He is one of the best long jumpers in Panama!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Biography Timeline" and actively mediate information for their peers as described in Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Message:</b> Can the student simplify complex information effectively for peers? (Specific Objective)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Gestures/Actions:</b> Does the student use appropriate gestures/actions to convey meaning and clarify misunderstandings? (Learning Outcome)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Visual Aids:</b> Does the student utilize their timeline visuals to aid clarification? (Learning Outcome)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Language:</b> Does the student use target vocabulary (athlete, achievement, award, win, more, best) and grammar (past simple, comparatives/superlatives, present continuous for future events) correctly?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Is pronunciation of '-ed' and '-ing' verb endings clear?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you said that helped your peers understand your famous Panamanian better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you use gestures or visuals? How did they help?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to simplify messages and clarify misunderstandings when you talk to others?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of explaining your Panamanian athlete to your group, and how did you try to make it clearer?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively simplify messages and clarify misunderstandings using gestures and visuals? Were the task demands appropriate for A2.2?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies worked well for mediation? Did the integration of the Biography Timeline project enhance the mediation skills?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation activities, especially regarding pronunciation or more complex clarifications?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Famous Panamanians: My Favorite Athletes!" theme by consolidating all language skills through mediation and project presentation. The next theme will introduce a new scenario and set of skills, building upon the communicative confidence gained here.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>